                <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
                <h3>Q1: How long is the flight from Newark to Hawaii?</h3>
                <p>United's seasonal nonstop from Newark to Honolulu spans approximately 10 hours 35 minutes to 11 hours 20 minutes. Connecting itineraries through San Francisco or Los Angeles extend total journey time to 13-16 hours, depending on layover duration.</p>

                <h3>Q2: Which airlines fly nonstop from Newark to Hawaii?</h3>
                <p>United Airlines operates seasonal nonstop service from Newark to Honolulu during peak winter months, typically December through March. Year-round travel requires connections through San Francisco, Los Angeles, Denver, or Seattle.</p>

                <h3>Q3: Do I need a passport to fly from Newark to Hawaii?</h3>
                <p>No passport required. Hawaii is a domestic US destination. A standard government-issued photo ID (driver's license or REAL ID) is sufficient for all passengers 18 and older. TSA regulations apply to any domestic flight.</p>

                <h3>Q4: What is the cheapest time to fly from Newark to Hawaii?</h3>
                <p>September and October historically offer the lowest fares, with economy round-trips often available at $550-$900. Winter months from December through March command premium pricing. Book 60-90 days ahead for peak season value.</p>

                <h3>Q5: How do I travel between Hawaiian Islands?</h3>
                <p>Hawaiian Airlines and Southwest operate frequent inter-island flights from Honolulu. Book early during peak season. No passenger ferry service currently operates between major islands; air travel is the only practical option.</p>

                <h3>Q6: Can I bring surfboards on flights to Hawaii?</h3>
                <p>Yes, all carriers accept surfboards. Southwest includes two free checked bags that can include surfboards - an exceptional value. United charges $150 each way for surfboards-proper padding and advance notification required.</p>

                <h3>Q7: What should I pack for Hawaii?</h3>
                <p>Light breathable clothing, swimwear, reef-safe sunscreen, sturdy sandals, hiking shoes for volcanic terrain, light rain jacket for windward sides, and modest attire for temple visits. Power adapter unnecessary - Hawaii uses standard US outlets.</p>

                <h3>Q8: Is Hawaii safe for tourists?</h3>
                <p>Hawaii is among the safest US destinations. Standard precautions apply regarding ocean safety (rip currents, shore breaks), sun exposure, and trail conditions. Violent crime is rare. Emergency services responsive. Ocean awareness more critical than crime awareness.</p>

                <h3>Q9: Do I need a car in Hawaii?</h3>
                <p>Essential for Maui, Big Island, and Kauai. On Oahu, Waikiki and Honolulu are accessible by bus and rideshare; a rental car is recommended for North Shore, Windward Coast, and Pearl Harbor. Book rental cars well in advance during peak season.</p>

                <h3>Q10: What is the best way to avoid jet lag when flying from Newark to Hawaii?</h3>
                <p>The six-hour time difference requires adjustment. Upon arrival, resist napping; expose yourself to sunlight; stay active until local evening. Hydration critical during flight. Morning departures from Newark, arriving afternoon Hawaii optimal for circadian alignment.</p>










